      "7": "7. Jesus Must Have the Preeminence\n\nScripture ultimately leads us to Jesus. We read Scripture to know and encounter Him—to find life in Him, be transformed into His image, walk with Him in obedience, and give Him the preeminence in all things. We do not merely want to know more about Jesus; we want to know Jesus.",
      "8": "8. Open the Tupperware Container\n\nAccurate interpretation matters. We must first understand what the text actually says in its historical, grammatical, literary, and biblical context. But that understanding is the container, not the final destination. Inside the container are the Spirit and life God intends His Word to impart.\n\nMastering the Bible seeks to open the Tupperware container-to move from accurate explanation into the spiritual reality the text reveals. We ask not only, “What does this passage mean?” but also, “What is God revealing here, and how does this truth lead us into greater knowledge of Him, relationship with Him, and life in Him?”",
      "9": "9. Read Scripture Through the Right Lens\n\nA lens determines how we see what is already there. Scripture provides the light, but the lens through which we approach it profoundly affects what we see.\n\nOur governing lens is the character and eternal purpose of God: God is infinitely good and holy; Scripture reveals His desire to dwell with humanity; Jesus stands at the center of His purposes; and the Spirit uses the Word to draw us into relationship with God and transform us into the likeness of Christ.\n\nThe lens never replaces sound interpretation or changes what the text says. It helps us see each passage within the larger reality of who God has revealed Himself to be and what He is accomplishing throughout Scripture.",
      "10": "10. Eat From the Tree of Life\n\nFrom the beginning, Scripture presents us with two trees—the tree of the knowledge of good and evil and the tree of life. They picture two fundamentally different ways of approaching God and His Word.\n\nWe can read Scripture merely to accumulate knowledge: facts to know, doctrines to argue, rules to follow, principles to apply, and lists of what is good and what is bad. Much of that information may be true, yet knowledge by itself is not the ultimate purpose of Scripture.\n\nOr we can come to the Word to receive life.\n\nJesus said, “The words that I speak to you are spirit, and they are life.” Scripture is intended to reveal God, communicate His heart, draw us into relationship with Jesus through the Holy Spirit, transform us into His likeness, and teach us to live from Him.\n\nMastering the Bible therefore values knowledge deeply, but refuses to stop there. We study the Word so that through what we understand, we may know the One who gave it. We do not merely study the tree—we eat its fruit."
